#
# The ARMADA's management link to the coprocessor, mvmgmt0, made an interface of this firewall at a
# boot that has it and nothing assigned to it (scripts/npuctl/mgmt-link.sh early): the assignment
# install.sh makes when it runs with the link there, on an appliance already running its driver. An
# appliance installed from a ready-made image runs install.sh at its first boot from the stick, where
# no driver is loaded yet, so there was no mvmgmt0 to assign, and until 2026-11-06 nothing assigned it
# later; nor after a factory reset, whose configuration has no such assignment. The link had no
# address: the bootup hook tried to tell the coprocessor its address over the console eleven times a
# boot and was never answered over the link, every reader of the coprocessor went over its console, and
# npu-clock.sh and npu-log.sh, which use the link alone, never set the coprocessor's clock or had its
# log sent here (the review's A4).
#
# HERE, AND NOT LATER IN THE BOOT. After 07-npuep: the driver makes mvmgmt0 at its attach, inside that
# hook's kldload and before the front ports, so at this point of every boot the driver attaches at, the
# link is there. And before rc.bootup, which checks that every interface it has assigned exists, and
# when one it does not take as volatile is missing, asks on the console for the interfaces to be
# assigned again and, with nobody there, assigns them itself: an assignment of oxmgmt0 while its driver
# made it a minute after that check wiped every other assignment of the second XGS 3300 (2026-10-23).
# So the assignment is written only at a boot where the link is there before the check; and a boot
# whose driver does not attach has neither mvmgmt0 nor a front port, which xgs_devices() declares
# volatile, all of them, so that boot asks for no assignment. rc.bootup then gives the link its address
# and loads the rules that admit the coprocessor itself, in the same boot, with nothing reloaded after
# it. After 30-xgs-assign, so that at a first boot the front ports take their places first, as on a
# box installed by hand.
#
# Nothing is done where the link is assigned already, or not there, and every path exits 0. Not on the
# XGS 3300, whose oxmgmt0 is down until its coprocessor is ready and is assigned by the bootup hook once
# started: install.sh puts this file on the ARMADA alone, and mgmt-link.sh does nothing here on any
# other family.

L=/usr/local/opnsense/scripts/npuctl/mgmt-link.sh
[ -f "${L}" ] || exit 0
/bin/sh "${L}" early
exit 0
